  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nConfidence remains high that quiet and dry conditions will\npersist through late Saturday under high pressure.\n\nHurricane Isaias is favored to weaken but maintain a closed\nsurface to mid-level closed low structure as it lifts northward \nthrough the Tennessee River Valley Saturday before advancing\neast northeast across the Upper Ohio River Valley to Mid \nAtlantic regions Sunday. Trough and low pressure ascent \ncombined with a tropical airmass (which pushes PWAT values \nbetween the 90th and 100th percentiles) results in widespread \nrain enveloping the region overnight Saturday through early \nMonday morning, with ensemble mean rainfall totals generally\nranging between 1 to 1.5 inches.  \n\nThe first concern relates to flooding risks where localized jet\ndynamics create narrow corridors of enhanced rainfall rates \nthat push totals into the 2-3\" range. Latest guidance favors \nsuch corridor developing along a SSW-NNE axis from roughly Noble\nCounty, OH towards Mercer County, PA (though variance remains \nin both its development and east-west positioning). If most of \nthis rain can occur within a 6-hr period (probability is less \nthan 10% for such to occur) and/or wind-aided debris (like \nleaves, twigs, etc.) clogs storm sewer water mitigation systems,\nthen localized flooding could occur. However, the preceding dry\nantecedent conditions combined with low probabilities for high \nrainfall rates (due to limited buoyancy) and/or accumulations \n(6-hr FFG for most areas is above 2\") means the likelihood of \nits development is low.\n\nThe other concern relates to the likely development of a\nstronger low-level southeasterly jet late Saturday into Sunday \nearly afternoon that results in gusty winds that approach Wind \nAdvisory criteria in the higher terrain. This 850mb jet, as\ndepicted by the 12z RRFS model, sits between 40-55 kts and \npeaks Sunday morning before waning, offering a chance for gusts\nif any mixing can occur. Though this is unlikely for the lower \nelevation locations (where prob of gusts exceeding 40mph are \nless than 10%), the higher terrain offers better chances purely \ndue to closer proximity to this jet such that probabilities of \nexceeding 40mph ar around 50-80% but only 20-40% for exceeding\n45mph. If confidence increases that gusts could meet/exceed \nWind Advisory criteria (46 mph), then a Wind Advisory for those \nhigher terrain areas would be issued.\n\nAll impacts wane Sunday night as the system exits east, though\nlingering rain may continue through Monday for the higher\nterrain. The next chance for rain arrives with a potential cold\nfront Tuesday afternoon/evening favoring northwest PA.",
